# End-to-end journeys

These drive a real browser against the real binary and a real database. That
is the point: a journey proved against a mock is a journey proved against the
mock.

It is also why they are not part of `pnpm check`. They need a database, they
take minutes rather than seconds, and they are the slowest and most brittle
layer of the pyramid — so this suite covers *journeys*, the paths where a
defect means nobody can use the application at all, and nothing else.
Everything else belongs in the component suite, which also runs in a browser
and takes seconds.

## Running them

```sh
# Once: download the browser.
pnpm exec playwright install chromium

# A database, and the environment the application needs.
make -C .. -f Makefile compose-up   # or your own PostgreSQL
export DATABASE_URL=...

pnpm test:e2e
```

By default the config builds and starts the application itself. Point
`APP_BASE_URL` at an instance you are already running to skip that:

```sh
APP_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080 pnpm test:e2e
```

## What belongs here

A journey earns a place here if its failure means nobody can use the
application: signing in, the shell rendering, a resource being created and
read back. A journey does not earn a place here because it is hard to unit
test.

Write assertions about what a person experiences — a form, a refusal that says
what to do, a page they end up on — rather than about the calls underneath.
The calls are already covered by the transport tests; what no unit test can
see is that the pieces are wired to each other.
